# Cloudflare Pages rewrites.
#
# NOTE ON REWRITE TARGETS: never point one at /index.html. Pages strips the
# .html extension from a target the same way it does from a request path, so
# `/noc /index.html 200` becomes a 308 to / and the rewrite never happens.
# That shipped once: every simulator dashboard and the whole legacy site
# redirected to the home page. The target is `/`.
#
# This file used to be one line: `/* /index.html 200`. That is the standard
# single-page-app fallback, and on this site it was actively harmful.
#
# Every real route here is prerendered to its own index.html by
# script/prerender.ts, so the fallback was not needed to serve them. What it
# did instead was answer 200 OK to every URL that does not exist. Google calls
# that a soft 404: maxdoubin.com/asdf returned a page, so the crawler treated
# a bottomless supply of junk URLs as real, spent crawl budget on them, and
# had to guess which of the 331 real pages were worth indexing. A 404 that
# says 200 is one of the few things that will stop a small site being indexed
# properly.
#
# So the fallback is now enumerated. Only routes that have NO prerendered file
# are rewritten to the shell; everything else is served as its own static
# document, and anything matching neither falls through to 404.html, which
# Cloudflare Pages serves with a real 404 status.
#
# scripts-ci/check-spa-routes.mjs fails the build if a route in App.tsx is
# neither prerendered nor listed here, so adding a route without adding it in
# one of those two places cannot ship a page that 404s at people.

# One site, one hostname: NOT SOLVABLE IN THIS FILE.
#
# www.maxdoubin.com answers 200, so every page exists at two addresses. The
# obvious fix was tried here and does not work:
#
#     https://www.maxdoubin.com/* https://maxdoubin.com/:splat 301
#
# Cloudflare Pages matches _redirects on the path only. An absolute URL in the
# source column is not a host matcher, it is just a path that never matches,
# so the rule sat here doing nothing while appearing to solve the problem.
# Verified against production after deploying it: www still answered 200.
#
# The real fix is outside the repository, in the Cloudflare dashboard. Until
# somebody applies it, the canonical tag on every page points at the apex,
# which is what actually keeps Google consolidating the two hosts, and it is
# doing that job today. Verified: https://www.maxdoubin.com/contact serves
# <link rel="canonical" href="https://maxdoubin.com/contact">.
#
# The steps, so this does not have to be worked out again:
#
#   1. DNS: www must be a PROXIED record, or Cloudflare never sees the
#      request and cannot redirect it. An A record for www to 192.0.2.0, or
#      AAAA to 100::, both reserved placeholders for originless setups. The
#      orange cloud is the part that matters; the address is never reached.
#
#   2. Rules > Redirect Rules > Create rule, on the maxdoubin.com zone:
#        If:   hostname equals www.maxdoubin.com
#        Then: dynamic redirect
#              target   concat("https://maxdoubin.com", http.request.uri.path)
#              status   301
#              preserve query string: on
#
#      Hostname only. Matching on the full URI would also catch the apex and
#      loop it into itself.
#
#   3. Check with curl, not a browser, because a browser follows and hides it:
#        curl -sSI https://www.maxdoubin.com/contact | head -3
#      Expect 301 and location: https://maxdoubin.com/contact. Then confirm
#      the apex still answers 200 rather than redirecting to itself.
#
# The alternative, if www is not wanted at all, is to remove www as a custom
# domain on the Pages project. That makes www stop resolving rather than
# redirect, which loses any link equity pointing at it, so the redirect is
# the better of the two.

# The simulator's internal dashboards used to be rewritten here, on the
# reasoning that they are canvas-driven with nothing meaningful to prerender
# and are noindex in _headers anyway. Both halves were true and the conclusion
# was still wrong.
#
# A 200 rewrite to / does not serve an empty page, it serves the home page.
# A crawler asking for /noc got the home page's title, the home page's h1 and
# a rel=canonical pointing at the home page. Google calls serving the home
# page in place of a page of its own a soft 404, and Search Console duly
# reported soft 404s on this site. The noindex did not prevent that; it made
# it sharper, because a noindex on a URL whose canonical points at a different
# URL is the one pairing Google warns can transfer the noindex to the
# canonical target, and the target was the home page.
#
# They are prerendered now, each with its own title, its own canonical and
# noindex on the page. A rule here would be dead weight: Pages serves the
# prerendered file and never reaches it.
#
# /game was already prerendered: it is one of the most distinctive things here
# and deserves a real shot at ranking.

# The pre-redesign site, redirected to the pages that replaced it.
#
# These were 200 rewrites to /, which meant an old inbound link did not reach
# the old page or the new one: it got the home page's document, at the old
# URL, with the home page's canonical. Google reported that as a soft 404,
# and the noindex in _headers made it worse rather than better, because a
# noindex on a URL whose canonical points elsewhere is the pairing that can
# carry the noindex over to the canonical target, which was the home page.
#
# A 301 to the current equivalent is better on every axis. Somebody following
# a years-old link lands on the article they were promised instead of the
# front page, the link's authority transfers to the page that now holds that
# content, and the duplicate URL stops existing rather than being papered
# over with a noindex.
#
# The post mapping is one to one and total: the legacy blog rendered the same
# post data as /blog, and all 247 slugs in postIndex.ts have a prerendered
# /blog/<slug>. Checked, not assumed.
#
# Ordered specific to general, because Pages takes the first match.
/legacy/blog/*   /blog/:splat  301
/legacy/blog     /blog         301
/legacy/projects /projects     301
/legacy/contact  /contact      301
/legacy/game     /game         301
/legacy          /             301

